"fix(planner): plan an inline property map once, instead of twice (#2390)

* Lower inline pattern attrs unconditionally, and only once

`MATCH (a {x: 1})` is a predicate, and the planner is the only thing that
represents it for most operators. Two gaps in that lowering:

The endpoint blocks skipped any alias already in `visited`. `CondTraverse`
covers that at runtime by re-evaluating `rp.from.attrs` / `rp.to.attrs` per
row, but `CondVarLenTraverse` and `AllShortestPaths` only ever evaluate the
edge's attrs, so for those operators nothing enforced the predicate:

    CREATE (n1:N {x: 1}), (n2:N {x: 2}), (n3:N {x: 3}),
           (n1)-[:R]-&gt;(n2), (n2)-[:R]-&gt;(n3)

    MATCH (a:N) WITH a
    MATCH (a {x: 1})-[:R*1..2]-&gt;(b)
    RETURN b.x ORDER BY b.x

returned 2, 3, 3 =E2=80=94 the third row starts at `n2`, which does not sat=
isfy
`x =3D 1`. Same for the destination endpoint and for allShortestPaths.

Going the other way, several branches lowered the same endpoint twice: once
onto the scan they build and again above the operator, because `visited` is
only updated after both. `push_filters_down` merges the stack into
`And(p, p)`, which no index can serve, so the query drops to a full scan.

Lower both endpoints unconditionally, in one place per operator, and route
every call through `lower_inline_attrs`, which emits a given (alias, attrs
map) at most once. Identity rather than equality is the right test: the
binder builds a fresh enriched clone per occurrence that writes its own
attrs, so `(a {x:1})--&gt;(c), (a {x:2})--&gt;(d)` carries two distinct map=
s for
one alias and both must be lowered.

The surviving copy is the one above the operator, not the one on the scan =
=E2=80=94
`select_scan_node` and `select_var_len_scan_node` prune and rebuild scan
subtrees, and `push_filters_down` lands the predicate back on the scan
afterwards.

Inline attrs on a CREATE or MERGE pattern are a constructor, not a
predicate. Those patterns come from `QueryGraph::filter_visited` and never
reach this path, so `create.rs`'s property template and `merge.rs`'s lookup
hash key are untouched.

tests/flow/test_inline_pattern_attributes.py covers the three shapes that
returned wrong rows, plus the shapes that only `CondTraverse`'s per-row
re-check enforces today =E2=80=94 those become load-bearing when the re-che=
ck goes.

* Stop endpoint attrs from gating the batched traverse path

`batched_eligible` required all three inline-attr maps =E2=80=94 the edge's=
 and both
endpoints' =E2=80=94 to be empty before a single-hop CondTraverse could tak=
e the
F=C2=B7A path. For the endpoints that was conservative rather than necessar=
y:
the planner now lowers both of them into a Filter unconditionally, so the
predicate is enforced whichever path runs, and expand_row's own check on
them is redundant with it.

The edge's attrs still gate it, and must. With `emit_relationship` false,
expand_batch binds one representative edge per (src, dst) pair, so a
predicate that tells parallel edges apart has to run during the per-row
scan, which iterates all of them.

Qualifying traverses move off expand_row =E2=80=94 three ExprEval tree walk=
s per
row plus per-pair attribute lookups =E2=80=94 and onto one delta_lmxm. Meas=
ured
over the bench corpus, instructions, median of three:

    reversed chain mid filter   54,967,241 -&gt; 21,069,847   2.61x
    rereferenced node attrs        928,448 -&gt;  1,031,579   0.90x

The second is the cost of the frontier matrix and mxm on a one-row
frontier (`a.id =3D 1` binds a single node), where expanding the row
directly was cheaper. It is 103k instructions against 33.9M saved on the
first, so it is left alone; a cardinality-aware choice between the two
paths would recover it and is worth doing on its own terms.

Queries whose edges carry inline attrs are unchanged, as expected:
traverse inline rel attrs 56.71M -&gt; 56.74M.

* Reach a MERGE branch's scan through IncludePending for index pushdown

`match_scan_with_filter` required the `Filter` to be the scan's immediate
parent. A MERGE match branch is `Filter -&gt; IncludePending -&gt; Scan`
(`Planner::set_include_pending_on_scans`, and `make_scan_subtree` in
`select_scan_node`), and `push_filters_down` will not descend through
`IncludePending`, so the adjacency never held there and filter pushdown
never fired for MERGE. Only the inline-attr path could serve those scans,
and that path handles a single equality attribute =E2=80=94 no ranges, no
multi-attribute conjunctions.

Look through a single-child `IncludePending`, and address the `Filter` by
its own index rather than as the scan's parent.

The `Filter` cannot be removed when it is reached this way, though.
`IncludePending` unions in nodes created earlier in the same query, which
are by construction absent from the index, so the index scan never filtered
them. Dropping the filter lets them through unchecked:

    MERGE (a:P {age: 40}) MERGE (b:P {age: 41})

created one node instead of two =E2=80=94 `b`'s index scan found nothing,
`IncludePending` supplied the pending `a`, and with no filter left to
re-check it, `b` matched `a`. So `over_pending` forces the post-filter on;
the index still narrows the scan, and the filter re-checks what it yields.

This is what lets the inline-attr pushdown path be removed once inline
attrs stop being carried on the pattern.

* Strip node inline attrs from the pattern once they are lowered

`IR::Filter` is now the only representation of a MATCH pattern's node inlin=
e
attributes. The planner lowers them and embeds a stripped `QueryNode` in th=
e
operator, so nothing downstream can re-derive the predicate or disagree wit=
h
it.

CREATE and MERGE patterns are untouched. Their `QueryGraph` comes from
`filter_visited` on the original, and `IR::Merge`'s create pattern is built
before `plan_match` runs, so `create.rs`'s property template and
`merge.rs`'s lookup hash key still see their attrs. Only the endpoints are
stripped, not the relationship's own attrs: `ExpandInto`,
`CondVarLenTraverse` and `AllShortestPaths` get no edge-attr Filter from th=
e
planner, and their runtime `rp.attrs` read is the only thing enforcing
`-[{k: 1}]-&gt;`.

The four consumers that read attrs instead of the plan:

- `make_scan_subtree` re-derived the filter from the node it was rebuilding=
.
  It now salvages the `Filter` operators off the pruned subtree's spine
  (`filters_of`, the sibling of `argument_leaf_of`) =E2=80=94 but only wher=
e it
  rebuilds the *same* endpoint. On the two chain-reversal paths the pruned
  filters constrain the old endpoint, which the reversed traverse binds
  rather than scans, so moving them onto the new scan would evaluate an
  unbound variable.
- `select_scan_node`'s var-length reversal guard cross-checked attrs agains=
t
  the Filters. With one representation there is nothing to cross-check.
- `utilize_index`'s inline-attr path is gone. It handled a single equality
  attribute; filter pushdown handles ranges and multi-attribute conjunction=
s,
  and since the previous commit it reaches MERGE branches too.
- `cond_traverse` evaluated `rp.from.attrs` and `rp.to.attrs` per row and
  re-checked them per pair, which is now three `ExprEval` walks over empty
  maps.

`fuse_anonymous_traverse`'s intermediate veto was an attrs probe backed by =
a
structural accident: a Filter on the intermediate breaks the parent/child
adjacency the pass requires, so fusion was blocked without the veto ever
being consulted. It is now a plan-wide check that nothing still references
the alias, which is the actual requirement.

Endpoint scoring needed splitting to survive the strip. `collect_filtered_v=
ars`
only ever looked above the chain, so an endpoint whose Filter sat mid-chain
scored as unconstrained; sweeping downwards as well fixes that but made
`MATCH (A:L {v:1})--&gt;(B)--&gt;(C), (B)--&gt;(D:L {v:1})` tie, and the ti=
e fell
through to &quot;nearer the leaf&quot;, losing the early filter. `FilteredV=
ars` keeps
the two apart: both count as constrained, and a predicate that currently ru=
ns
*after* the whole traversal wins the tie, because scanning from there is wh=
at
moves it to the front.

Instructions, median of two, single-query runs:

    traverse inline rel attrs   56,734,497 -&gt; 38,539,283   1.47x
    rereferenced node attrs      1,031,762 -&gt;    917,229   1.12x
    reversed 2hop chain         19,457,298 -&gt; 19,195,065   1.01x
    reversed chain mid filter   21,062,389 -&gt; 20,778,506   1.01x
    cartesian split filter       1,694,529 -&gt;  1,694,995   1.00x

The first is the per-row endpoint evaluation going away: that query keeps i=
ts
edge attrs, so it stays on `expand_row`, which was evaluating three attribu=
te
maps per row to check two empty ones.

* Strip edge inline attrs; every operator now lowers its own

Completes the single representation. `MATCH ()-[{k: 1}]-&gt;()` no longer
reaches the runtime as pattern data.

The four operators that can carry an edge pattern enforce it differently, s=
o
they get it in different forms:

- `CondTraverse` and `ExpandInto` take a `Filter` above them, plus a new
  `edge_predicate` flag. The flag is what makes the Filter safe: with
  `emit_relationship` false these ops collapse a (src, dst) pair to one
  representative edge, and a predicate would then be tested against an
  arbitrary member of the group, dropping rows whose match is a sibling.
  `ExpandInto` had no edge-attr lowering at all before this.
- `CondVarLenTraverse` and `AllShortestPaths` take it in `edge_filter`,
  applied per edge during the walk. A Filter above those could only reject =
an
  assembled path, which is a different question. `AllShortestPaths` gains t=
he
  field; it mirrors CVLT, reusing one env row and overwriting the alias slo=
t
  per edge rather than cloning.

`edge_predicate` is a third fact, not a synonym for either neighbour.
`emit_relationship` is row multiplicity; `bind_relationship` (#2428) is
whether anything reads the edge. `MATCH p =3D (a)-[:R]-&gt;(b) RETURN p` bi=
nds its
edge and still must collapse, so neither can stand in for &quot;are paralle=
l edges
distinguishable here&quot;.

`fuse_anonymous_traverse` refuses to fuse a hop carrying `edge_predicate`: =
a
fused chain binds no per-hop edge, so there would be nothing to test.

Instructions, median of two, single-query runs:

    expand-into inline rel attrs   39,821,761 -&gt; 20,635,588   1.93x
    traverse inline rel attrs      38,539,283 -&gt; 42,809,251   0.90x

The regression is the CondTraverse case and it is structural: the inline
check pruned during edge iteration, whereas the Filter runs after the batch
is gathered, so rejected rows are built before being dropped. ExpandInto
gains far more than CondTraverse loses because its per-edge check did a
string-keyed attribute lookup per candidate. Giving both ops an `edge_filte=
r`
expression like CVLT's, evaluated during iteration, would recover it and
unify all four on one mechanism =E2=80=94 worth doing, but it is a design c=
hange
rather than part of this one.

* Assert in debug builds that no pattern still carries inline attrs

The previous commits removed every consumer that re-derived an inline
predicate from the pattern. This keeps them removed: `optimize` now walks t=
he
finished plan and panics if any match-side operator still carries attrs.

It guards a failure mode that is invisible in results. A second copy of the
predicate does not change what a query returns =E2=80=94 it changes what th=
e plan can
be rewritten into. `push_filters_down` merged the duplicates into
`And(p, p)`, no index could serve that shape, and the query silently fell
back to a scan. Nothing failed; it just got slower.

Create and Merge are exempt and must stay so: there the attrs are the
property template for the entity being built.

Verified in both directions =E2=80=94 the assertion holds across the debug-=
mode flow
suites, and with `strip_node_attrs` stubbed to a no-op it fires:

    FalkorDB panic: panicked at graph/src/planner/optimizer/mod.rs:208:9:
    traverse still carries inline pattern attrs after planning; they must b=
e
    lowered exactly once (Filter, or edge_filter for the walks) and strippe=
d:
    Conditional Traverse | (a)-&gt;(b)

`#[cfg(debug_assertions)]`, with a no-op in release, so it costs nothing in
shipped builds =E2=80=94 and correspondingly only guards CI and local debug=
 runs.

* Prune edge predicates during traversal instead of after the batch

Stripping edge attrs left `CondTraverse` enforcing them through a `Filter`
above itself, which meant every candidate row was built and then discarded.
`GRAPH.PROFILE` on `MATCH (a:Person)-[:KNOWS {weight: 1}]-&gt;(b) RETURN
count(b)`, over a graph where nothing matches:

    Conditional Traverse | Records produced: 10000 | 1.349 ms
    Filter               | Records produced: 0     | 0.289 ms

10,000 rows constructed for the Filter to throw away. The cost scaled with
the predicate's *selectivity* =E2=80=94 the more it rejected, the more was =
wasted =E2=80=94
which is backwards, since a selective edge predicate is the case worth
optimizing.

`absorb_edge_filters_into_traverse` moves an edge-only `Filter` into the
operator's new `edge_filter`, applied per candidate edge. Same profile now:

    Conditional Traverse | Records produced: 0     | 0.893 ms

faster than the per-edge attribute check this refactor replaced (1.114 ms),
because the predicate goes through the normal evaluator against a reused en=
v
row rather than a bespoke map comparison, and the Filter operator disappear=
s.

Two things constrain where it can run and what it can touch.

It runs *last*, after `utilize_index` and `reduce_bound_edge`. An inline ed=
ge
attr is index-servable =E2=80=94 `MATCH ()-[e:R{v:1}]-&gt;() RETURN e` beco=
mes an
`EdgeByIndexScan` (tests/flow/test_edge_index_scans.py) =E2=80=94 so absorb=
ing early
would hide the Filter from the pass that does that. Its sibling
`absorb_edge_filters_into_vlt` runs early for the opposite reason: a
var-length edge filter can never be indexed.

And it takes `CondTraverse` only. `FilterOp` evaluates simple predicates wi=
th
vectorized batch kernels, so absorbing trades those for a scalar eval per
edge; it wins only where the operator expands the row set and early rejecti=
on
avoids building rows. `ExpandInto` verifies an edge between two already-bou=
nd
endpoints, and absorbing there measured 20.6M -&gt; 26.3M instructions agai=
nst
`CondTraverse`'s 42.8M -&gt; 28.6M. So `ExpandInto` keeps its Filter and ca=
rries
`edge_predicate: bool` instead =E2=80=94 it still must not collapse a (src,=
 dst) pair
to one representative edge, or the Filter would test an arbitrary member of
the group. Dropping that flag on the assumption `edge_filter` had replaced =
it
made `MATCH (a)-[{k: 2}]-&gt;(b)` return 0 rows where one parallel edge mat=
ched.

Instructions, median of two, single-query runs, against the state before
edge attrs were stripped:

    traverse inline rel attrs      38,539,283 -&gt; 28,630,234   1.35x
    expand-into inline rel attrs   39,821,761 -&gt; 20,768,463   1.92x

* Lock in that filter merging leaves predicates alone

PR #2390 taught `push_filters_down` to drop duplicate conjuncts when mergin=
g
stacked filters. With inline attrs lowered once, the planner no longer emit=
s
the duplicate that motivated it, so that change is dropped in favour of the
invariant: `debug_assert_no_pattern_attrs` makes a second copy
unconstructible rather than collapsing it afterwards. Deduping would hide a
planner that had started producing them again.

Three tests, each stating only what it proves. Two of the guards I first
wrote for this turned out to be vacuous under ablation, so:

- `distinct_endpoint_attrs_both_survive` and
  `user_written_duplicate_is_not_touched` pin the merge point down: distinc=
t
  predicates both survive, and `WHERE a.p =3D 1 AND a.p =3D 1` =E2=80=94 le=
gal Cypher,
  and indistinguishable at the merge point from a planner duplicate, which =
is
  the other reason not to dedup there =E2=80=94 is left alone.
- `test14_inline_attr_is_served_by_the_index` asserts the payoff: the
  canonical shape reaches an index scan with no residual Filter.

None of them detects a returning duplicate, and the comments say so. On a
default build there is nothing to detect: emitting the endpoint filter twic=
e
produces a byte-identical plan, because `utilize_index` folds
`And(a.p =3D 1, a.p =3D 1)` back into one index query. It is observable onl=
y
under `--features index-falkordb`, where an unserviceable `And` is a hard
error =E2=80=94 which is where #2390 found it, and the only place a duplica=
te-guard
could live.

* Measure the edge-filter absorb, and stop its loop depending on a mutation

Review asked what the absorb actually buys, given it puts a predicate in th=
e
operator's IR rather than leaving it as a Filter op. Two answers.

## It buys the rejected rows

The query I first cited for this was a poor witness:
`MATCH (a:Person)-[:KNOWS {weight: 1}]-&gt;(b)` probes :KNOWS for a propert=
y no
:KNOWS edge carries, so it is unsatisfiable rather than 0%-selective, and i=
t
measures only the extreme.

A :RATED ring gives a real sweep at constant fan-out =E2=80=94 10,000 edges=
, `w`
spread 0..9 (1,000 each), plus a constant `c` so a predicate can match all =
of
them. Both configurations correct, batching disabled on both sides, differi=
ng
only in where the predicate runs. Instructions, median of three:

