e"><a data-pjax=3D"true" title=3D"fix: re-check pause and role before a wri=
te commits, not just at admission (#2450)

* fix: re-check pause and role before a write commits, not just at admissio=
n

Closes #2371

A graph write is admitted on the Redis main thread, where Redis has already
vetted the pause state and the role, but it mutates and replicates later fr=
om a
background thread. Nothing re-checked either condition in between, so a
CLIENT PAUSE / FAILOVER window opening in that gap - or a demotion to repli=
ca -
let the write proceed anyway and crash the master on propagateNow()'s invar=
iant:

    server.c:3600 '!(isPausedActions(PAUSE_ACTION_REPLICA) &amp;&amp;
                     (!server.client_pause_in_transaction))'

Redis's own comment there gives the reason: the dataset must be fixed durin=
g a
replica pause, or data may be lost during a failover.

Query write path (query_session.rs)
-----------------------------------
`upgrade_to_write` now re-validates under the GIL, immediately before mutat=
ing.
Holding the GIL is what makes it race-free: CLIENT PAUSE and role-change ev=
ents
both run on the main thread, which needs the GIL we hold, so neither can mo=
ve
between the check and the commit.

Mirrors C's QueryCtx_AcquireWriteLock (query_ctx.c, PR #2372): bypass on
LOADING / ASYNC_LOADING, then AvoidReplicaTraffic, then READONLY - delibera=
tely
READONLY rather than MASTER, so a replica running `replica-read-only no` st=
ill
accepts writes. Both messages are byte-identical to C's
EMSG_REPLICA_TRAFFIC_PAUSED / EMSG_NOT_MASTER so a client or test can match
either engine, and they are now `#[error(...)]` variants of one type rather=
 than
inline literals, since they are a compatibility contract.

No check for a role that flipped away and back: the only flip that matters
resynced, which frees and re-registers the graph key, so `graph_is_register=
ed`
has already aborted the write.

Telemetry flusher (telemetry.rs)
--------------------------------
The flusher replicates XADDs from a background thread, so it needs both che=
cks
too, and it had neither.

Pause: hold the batch and retry once the window closes, bounded by
DEFERRED_XADD_MAX with oldest-first drop - the stream is already lossy
(MAX_INFO_QUERIES trims it on every XADD), so bounding memory matters more =
than
keeping every entry across a long pause.

Role: discard the batch. `enqueue_entry` skips on replicas, but that runs o=
n the
producing thread and says nothing about what is true at dispatch. The pause=
 guard
is what makes this interleaving likely rather than exotic: a FAILOVER opens=
 the
window, the batch is held for its duration, and the window closes at the mo=
ment
this instance has become a replica - so the whole held batch would be dispa=
tched
from a replica, duplicating what the new master is already replicating to u=
s.

Bulk (bulk_insert.rs)
---------------------
`preauthorized` narrows from three flags to ASYNC_LOADING. #2420 and #2421 =
route
REPLICATED and LOADING inline, so neither reaches that worker any more. Thi=
s is
the collapse the previous comment predicted. ASYNC_LOADING stays: it is in =
C's
authorization bypass but in neither dispatcher predicate, so such a batch s=
till
spawns a worker, and that worker can escalate after the loading window clos=
es.

* refactor: replace the preauthorized flag with the two facts it collapsed

`preauthorized` was one boolean meaning &quot;skip both re-checks&quot;, ch=
osen by hand at
two call sites with no shared rule. It named the checks rather than the wri=
te, so
a call site could not answer it without knowing what the guard does, and th=
e two
conditions it merged have different triggers.

Split into the facts themselves:

    pub struct WriteFacts {
        pub replicates: bool,       // can reach the replication stream fro=
m here
        pub originated_here: bool,  // our decision, vs replaying someone e=
lse's
    }

The guard becomes a direct translation, each fact paired to the error it ra=
ises.
`begin` is `begin_with(graph, WriteFacts::CLIENT)`, so the six default site=
s are
untouched and only the two genuine exceptions spell themselves out.

This closes a leak the single boolean could not express: bulk under
ASYNC_LOADING replicates, but was skipping the pause check along with the r=
ole
check. It now waives only the role check =E2=80=94 narrower than C, whose a=
uthorization
bypass is wholesale on REPLICATED | LOADING | ASYNC_LOADING.

Both fields are independently load-bearing, ablated separately to prove it:
forcing `replicates` false fails test_pause_replication_race, forcing
`originated_here` true fails test_constraint.

LOADING cannot reach reauthorize_write =E2=80=94 `dispatch::must_run_inline=
` contains it,
so replay runs inline and never escalates. Now a debug_assert naming that
predicate instead of a silent bypass, so narrowing it surfaces here rather =
than
quietly changing which writes get authorized. ASYNC_LOADING is not in that
predicate and does reach the check, so it keeps its handling.

Reasoning moved out of the comments into docs/write-authorization.md: a sta=
te
diagram of master/replica/loading transitions with the pause called out as
orthogonal, a flowchart from dispatch to commit-or-abort, the two-question =
rule
for a new call site, and a coverage table. That table also documents the wr=
ite
path the comments never mentioned - GRAPH.CONSTRAINT, DELETE, RESTORE, UDF,=
 COPY
and EFFECT mutate and replicate inline with no session at all, authorized b=
y
Redis's own dispatch. Comments shrink to pointers: query_session.rs and
constraint.rs lose 170 lines between them.

Also drops SPAWN_ONLY_FILES. It was justified by a shared services containe=
r
across test files, but the services matrix is per test_file so each file al=
ready
gets its own; test_replication_states.py does slaveof in that bucket today.
test_matrix_split.py returns to matching main.

Review fixes: explicit imports over wildcards, assertGreater/assertLessEqua=
l over
assertTrue comparisons, an explanatory comment on the best-effort teardown =
catch,
the pause test's writers now collect unexpected errors instead of letting a=
n
adaptive count absorb them, and the telemetry test asserts its stream is
non-empty before pausing so it cannot pass vacuously.

Full flow suite 1415/1415; cargo test -p graph 139 passed; fmt clean; clipp=
y
unchanged from main." class=3D"Link--secondary" href=3D"https://github.com/=
