ssage"><a data-pjax=3D"true" title=3D"perf: read parameters straight into v=
alues, not an expression tree (6.5x) (#2451)

A parameter is a literal - `evaluate_param` accepts constants, lists,
maps and negation, and nothing else - but every one was parsed into an
`ExprIR` tree first and then walked twice: once in `get_plan` for the
optimizer, once in `graph_core` for the runtime.

For the payloads bulk loaders inline that tree is one node per literal.
A 6.7MB batch of 1477 maps, each with a 384-dim embedding, is ~567k
literals, and building their nodes was the whole query: profiling put
`parse_expr_inner`, `parse_primary_expr` and `parse_map` at the top with
`evaluate_param` barely registering.

`parse_param_value` now reads the common case straight into a `Value`.
Anything it does not recognise, or that turns out to be an expression
rather than a literal (`p=3D1+2`, `p=3D[1,2][0]`), is handed back to
`parse_expr` + `evaluate_param`, so those keep the result and the error
message they always had. `Plan::parameters` holds values now, which also
removes both of the walks.

Measured against the C module on that batch, 21 iterations:

    C (reference)     25.0ms median
    after             52.4ms median     2.10x slower than C
    before           341.4ms median    13.68x slower than C

6.5x on the query, and it moves the remaining distance from C from 13.7x
to 2.1x. What is left is lexing the literals themselves - `get_token`
and `lex_numeric` are now the top frames - and Rust already parses a
6.7MB query with no parameters in 7.0ms against C's 16.9ms.

Behaviour is pinned by 54 parameter cases run against the previous build
and this one: 53 identical, including `-true` being Null, i64::MIN
negating to itself, and every error message. The one difference is
`CYPHER p=3D RETURN $p`, which now reports &quot;Invalid parameter expressi=
on.&quot;
instead of failing later on the query body - a parameter that is not a
literal is now noticed while it is being read rather than after.

