  the only job with a legitimate skip =E2=80=94 it runs only when the toolc=
hain changed
  =E2=80=94 so it is the only one allowed to report anything but success. T=
he
  prerequisites it was missing (docker, flow-test-matrix) are in `needs` no=
w.

  check-files no longer pipes into `grep -q`. Under the `set -o pipefail` t=
his
  branch added, grep exits on the first match, git diff takes SIGPIPE, and =
the
  pipeline reports 141 =E2=80=94 so a real toolchain change read as changed=
=3Dfalse,
  non-deterministically, depending on whether the diff filled the pipe buff=
er.
  The diff is materialised to a file first.

  persist-credentials: false on every checkout, not just the two in
  _flow-flavour.yml. Twelve of them run pull-request code =E2=80=94 cargo, =
pytest,
  flow.sh, the matrix generator =E2=80=94 and under merge_group the token t=
hey were
  leaving in .git/config is the base repository's.

  timeout-minutes on every job. Not cancelling a queue entry is deliberate,=
 but
  it means a wedged one would otherwise hold the queue for the six-hour def=
ault.

  .dockerignore joins the toolchain trigger set; it changes the build conte=
xt.

  `test` downloads the engine instead of compiling it again. The Rust unit =
tests
  move into build-engine, which already has the compiled tree =E2=80=94 the=
y need the
  source, not the .so, which is why the job could not simply download befor=
e.

* ci: drop CODEOWNERS and the main-rs trunk

CODEOWNERS went in to force a maintainer to read a fork's Dockerfile before
queueing it. The check-files refusal added earlier in this branch removes t=
hat
path entirely, so the file lost its job in the same change that created it =
=E2=80=94
what was left was ordinary review policy, which this PR should not decide.

It would not have worked either: it referenced @FalkorDB/maintainers, and t=
he
org has one team, falkordb-devops. GitHub drops reviewer assignment for an
unknown team silently, so the file was decorative.

main-rs was the interim Rust branch during the migration; main is the trunk=
 now.
33 references across 9 hand-written workflows, all of which collapse: the
two-element trunk lists become a single ref, and two `if` chains that exist=
ed
only to fall back to main-rs when main was absent become one assignment. Th=
at
also removes a now-dead REF_NAME env in batch-backport.yml.

_flow-flavour.yml's header no longer states a bucket count. It said 97, thi=
s
branch corrected it to 108, and it would be wrong again shortly =E2=80=94 t=
he number
belongs in test_matrix_split.py, which computes it and now fails on it.

flaky-test-rca.lock.yml still mentions main-rs; it is generated from the .m=
d
beside it and is not edited by hand.

* ci: pass use_image as a single-line expression

A folded scalar keeps the newline when its continuation line is indented
deeper than the first, so `use_image` reached the boolean input as a
two-line string and GitHub rejected the workflow at startup =E2=80=94 the r=
un
showed startup_failure with no job and no annotation.

actionlint does not catch this: the YAML is valid and the expression is
well-formed. Only the input's declared type makes it wrong.

* ci: always supply test_files; a matrix expands before the job's if

flow-asan runs the image vehicle and does not use test_files, so it was lef=
t
to default to []. The artifact job in _flow-flavour.yml matrices over that
array, and GitHub expands a matrix BEFORE evaluating the job's `if:` =E2=80=
=94 an
empty vector is not a skipped job, it is a workflow that fails at startup.

Made the input required so the next caller cannot omit it by accident.

* ci: split the artifact vehicle into its own reusable workflow

A matrix is expanded before any job's `if:` is evaluated, so every matrix i=
n a
workflow run counts against the 256-cell budget whether or not its job runs=
.
Holding the image and artifact shapes in one _flow-flavour.yml meant a rele=
ase
call asked for 216 services + 18 spawn + 234 artifact =3D 468 cells, and Gi=
tHub
rejected the workflow at startup =E2=80=94 no jobs, no annotation, just
startup_failure.

That is the same constraint the per-flavour split already exists for, appli=
ed
one level down. _flow-artifact.yml now holds the artifact shape; _flow-flav=
our
keeps the image shape and loses the vehicle input it no longer needs. Each =
call
is back under budget: 234 for release-on-image, 117 for asan, 117 for artif=
act.

A job cannot pick its `uses:` at runtime, so the release flavour is two thi=
n
calls gated on use_image rather than one. The flavour's configuration is st=
ill
stated once per flavour, which was the point.

* ci: finish the timeout coverage

services, spawn and ci-gate were missed: the insertion pattern assumed the =
job
header ran name/runs-on with nothing between, and those three do not. Nothi=
ng
now inherits the six-hour default, which matters most for a queue entry, si=
nce
this workflow deliberately does not cancel one.

* ci: point the artifact vehicle at TARGET_DIR, not RELEASE

Every coverage-flow cell failed with 'Connection refused' on localhost:6379
half a second in =E2=80=94 RLTest never started redis, because it was hande=
d
--module target/release/libfalkordb.so while the coverage artifact had been
unpacked into target/debug.

The cause is a GitHub expression trap, not a logic error. I wrote

    RELEASE: ${{ inputs.collect_coverage &amp;&amp; '' || '1' }}

expecting a ternary. GHA's &amp;&amp; and || return values and the empty st=
ring is
FALSY, so the left side evaluates to '' and the || then replaces it: the
expression is always '1'. The job log shows RELEASE: 1 on the coverage cell=
s.

Setting TARGET_DIR from artifact_path removes the inference entirely =E2=80=
=94 flow.sh
honours TARGET_DIR ahead of RELEASE, and the directory the tests read is no=
w
the same input that decided where the download went.

Audited the other ternaries in these workflows: the rest have a truthy
then-branch, which is what makes the idiom safe.

* ci: drop the registry credential before the spawn cell runs PR code

The spawn job logs in to GHCR so the in-container docker can pull the RC im=
age,
then runs ./flow.sh =E2=80=94 test code from the pull request =E2=80=94 in =
the same workspace,
with the token still in the docker config where that code can read it.

persist-credentials: false covers the git token; this is the other one. The
image is local by then, so the login has no remaining purpose.

* ci: make ci-gate actually reject a masked failure

The earlier commit claimed this and did not deliver it: the scripted edit t=
hat
was meant to replace the predicate did not match, changed nothing, and I
reported it as done without re-reading the file. The gate still accepted
`skipped` for every dependency, which is exactly the hole it was supposed t=
o
close =E2=80=94 CodeRabbit re-checked and caught it.

`docker` is the only job with a legitimate skip, because it runs only when =
the
toolchain changed. Everything else must report success.

Verified against three inputs rather than by inspection:
  docker skipped, rest success              -&gt; pass
  docker failed, rest skipped               -&gt; fail
  docker success, one dependency skipped    -&gt; fail

* ci: promote and release from the queue's image, never the PR's

Same-repo pull requests still publish rc-pr-&lt;N&gt; =E2=80=94 that stays,=
 it is useful for
benchmark-cov and for pulling a PR's image by hand. What changes is that
nothing SHIPS it.

Two images exist per merged PR: rc-pr-&lt;N&gt; built from the PR head, and
rc-mg-&lt;sha&gt; built by the queue from the merge candidate. Promotion wa=
s taking
the first. That ships bytes which were never tested in the combination that
actually merged =E2=80=94 the staleness this whole change exists to remove,=
 surviving
in a new place. And a fork PR publishes no rc-pr-&lt;N&gt; at all, so the f=
irst fork
merge through the queue could not advance :edge.

rust-push.yml's promote-edge and toolchain promotion, and release-image.yml=
's
retag, now key on the commit SHA. find-pr and the RC_PR_N lookup are delete=
d
rather than rewritten: both existed because squash merges produced SHAs tha=
t
were never built, and the queue tests the merge candidate, so that premise =
is
false.

This rests on the merge-group SHA being the SHA that lands, which is verifi=
ed
under SQUASH against another repository but not yet here. The failure mode =
is
benign and loud: a mismatch means imagetools cannot find the source tag and
:edge stops advancing, rather than shipping something wrong.

Also deletes flaky-test-rca.lock.yml, per review. It is generated, and it h=
as
never succeeded =E2=80=94 15 failures and 25 skips across its last 40 runs,=
 the most
recent today. flaky-test-rca.md, its source, is left in place.

* ci: restore CODEOWNERS, and close three gaps review found

CODEOWNERS is back, with the error that made it useless last time fixed: it
named @FalkorDB/maintainers, which does not exist. The org has one team,
falkordb-devops (3 members), and GitHub drops reviewer assignment for an
unknown team silently, so the file was decorative.

It is the right control here for a reason the workflow check cannot match: =
a
merge-queue run reads the workflow from the queue branch, which carries the
pull request's own changes, so a fork can edit the step meant to refuse it.=
 The
CODEOWNERS that applies is always the base branch's, so a fork cannot suppl=
y
the rules that judge it. It now also lists /flow.sh and itself, per review;
weakening it needs the same approval it grants.

It only REQUESTS review on its own. Blocking needs &quot;Require review fro=
m Code
Owners&quot; in the ruleset, which is a separate decision.

Three gaps closed alongside:

  pull_request now carries branches-ignore master. Dropping the trunk filte=
r to
  give stacked PRs engine CI also pointed this workflow at the C tree, whic=
h has
  no Cargo workspace; 26 open pull requests would have started failing.

  The fork refusal runs under merge_group. It was gated on the event being
  pull_request, so it did not fire in the queue, which is precisely where t=
he
  code is about to run with the base repository's token. The queued PR is
  resolved from the ref and its head repo checked; anything unresolvable fa=
ils
  closed.

  The refusal matches the same paths the toolchain trigger watches, plus
  .github/. It was only blocking build/ and .github/, so graphblas.sh,
  redisearch.sh, deps/RediSearch, .gitmodules and .dockerignore each set
  file_changed true, started the privileged docker job, and failed on the d=
enied
  push instead of saying why.

* ci: scope CODEOWNERS to build and .github, owned by falkordb-core

Drops /flow.sh: it executes, but a contributor has to be able to adjust how=
 a
test runs, and that exposure is covered by the queue running with
persist-credentials off and no registry credential rather than by review.

* ci: fix the comment above ci-gate

The &quot;Image-free tier&quot; banner headed the five inline jobs that mov=
ed into
_ci-pipeline.yml. They are gone, so it sat above ci-gate describing nothing=
 and
claiming that everything below it builds libfalkordb.so, which ci-gate does=
 not.

Replaced with what ci-gate is actually for: the single name to put in the
ruleset, why it has to be an aggregate rather than the job names themselves
(a fork runs the artifact vehicle only, so an image-vehicle job name would =
never
report on a fork and would block it forever), and why it carries
`if: !cancelled()`.

