rectory-commit-message"><a data-pjax=3D"true" title=3D"build: replace graph=
blas.sh/redisearch.sh with a native-deps crate (#2695)

* build: replace graphblas.sh/redisearch.sh with a native-deps crate

GraphBLAS and LAGraph were not tracked by git at all -- graphblas.sh cloned
them at build time from a GRAPHBLAS_VERSION string, so nothing tied a build=
 to
a reviewable commit and gen_prejit.sh had to grep the script to learn the
version. Add both as submodules alongside deps/RediSearch, so all three pin=
s
are gitlinks, and replace both shell scripts with a native-deps crate that =
owns
the recipes and a content-addressed artifact cache.

The cache key covers everything the artifacts are ABI-tied to: the submodul=
e
revision, the GB_control patch, the vendored PreJIT kernels, the recipe
sources, $CC/$CXX --version, the target triple and the OpenMP/sanitizer
flavour. Two consequences fall out of that:

  * graph/build.rs no longer sniffs RUSTFLAGS to guess whether an ASAN buil=
d is
    in progress. The sanitizer flavour is a key input, so the prefix it is
    handed is already the right one -- the variant-dir scan and pick(asan_b=
uild)
    heuristic are gone.
  * a compiler bump cannot silently reuse stale-ABI archives, which the old
    hashFiles(script) invalidation allowed.

native-deps is its own workspace, consumed as a path build-dependency. That
keeps it a plain function call rather than a nested cargo invocation, so it
cannot inherit RUSTFLAGS/CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS/CARGO_BUILD_TARGET from an
instrumented build of the outer workspace.

Restructure build/Dockerfile into deps-base -&gt; graphblas-build/redisearc=
h-build
-&gt; final so a dep bump and an apt tweak can no longer invalidate each ot=
her;
adding a pytest dependency used to invalidate a ~12 minute GraphBLAS build.
CC/CXX/LIBOMP_PREFIX are pinned as ENV in every image because they are key
inputs: if the final stage disagreed with the dep stage, every prebuilt
artifact would miss. The same hazard existed latent in Dockerfile.rhel9, wh=
ere
a bare `ARG CXX` shadowed the value the deps were built with.

Two behaviours worth recording, both found by running things rather than
reading them:

  * Config/GB_prejit.c is a tracked *generated* file; cmake rewrites it fro=
m
    whatever PreJIT/*.c it finds, so it must be in the SourceGuard snapshot=
 set
    or every build leaves the submodule dirty.
  * a submodule's .git is a gitdir: pointer that dangles in a Docker contex=
t,
    and git aborts outright next to a broken one -- which broke `git apply`=
 for
    the GraphBLAS patch. Deleting it is not enough either: RediSearch's
    ffi/build.rs walks up looking for a path where .git merely *exists*.
    recipes::ensure_git_root drops an empty .git directory, which satisfies
    both, and removes it afterwards.

deps/native-deps.lock mirrors the gitlinks as a plain file because a Docker
build context carries no .git for the dep stages to read; CI enforces the t=
wo
agree via `lock --check`.

Closes #2315

Co-authored-by: Copilot &lt;223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com&gt;

* fix: address review findings on the native-deps crate

Spec axis:

  * `lock --check` compared the RENDERED lock file, including `pin` -- whic=
h
    lock::from_git recovers with `git describe` run inside the submodule. T=
he
    lint job checks out without submodules (deliberately: cloning ~2GB to
    validate a text file is not worth it), so describe fails, falls back to=
 the
    short rev, and the gate rejected a perfectly correct lock file. It now
    compares path/url/rev, the fields that actually bind a build to a commi=
t;
    Entry::pin already documents itself as informational and not part of th=
e
    key. Verified both ways: passes with and without submodules, still reje=
cts
    a doctored rev.
  * REDISEARCH_SAN had no cross-check, so a sanitizer build that forgot to =
set
    it silently linked the CLEAN RediSearch archive into an instrumented
    module. graph/build.rs now refuses that combination. It reads the
    target-specific CARGO_TARGET_&lt;TRIPLE&gt;_RUSTFLAGS as well as RUSTFL=
AGS,
    because that is the form Dockerfile.asan actually uses -- checking only=
 the
    latter would have missed the exact case the guard is for. This is not t=
he
    RUSTFLAGS *sniffing* that was removed: nothing here decides anything, i=
t
    only rejects a combination that is definitely wrong.
  * Request::offline documented itself as &quot;set in the runtime Docker i=
mages&quot;
    when nothing sets it, asserting a guarantee the tree does not ship. The=
 doc
    now describes what is actually true and why.
  * The GRAPHBLAS_PREFIX/LAGRAPH_PREFIX/REDISEARCH_PREFIX overrides bypass =
the
    key entirely. Kept as a deliberate escape hatch, but taking one now log=
s a
    warning: the hazard this cache exists to prevent is *silent* stale-ABI
    reuse, and a quiet escape hatch is indistinguishable from the bug.
  * .devcontainer/README.md no longer hand-writes the GraphBLAS version, wh=
ich
    was a second record of a pin the gitlink owns.

Standards axis:

  * The PreJIT kernel predicate was duplicated between key.rs (what the cac=
he
    key covers) and recipes/graphblas.rs (what actually gets vendored). Tho=
se
    two must agree or the key stops describing the artifact -- the precise
    stale-ABI hit the design prevents. Extracted as util::is_prejit_kernel =
so
    drift is impossible.
  * Imported the items used at call sites rather than writing fully-qualifi=
ed
    paths, per CLAUDE.md's Code Style section: capture_opt in toolchain.rs,
    PathBuf/sha256_hex/capture in main.rs, OsStr in recipes/mod.rs.
  * Dropped Toolchain::jobs (written, never read) and the Dep::parse aliase=
s
    (gb/rs/search/graph_blas/la_graph) that USAGE never documented.

Co-authored-by: Copilot &lt;223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com&gt;

* refactor: collapse KeyContext into Ctx

Review flagged these as a Data Clump and a long parameter list. Both types =
were
introduced by this branch and nothing outside it depends on them, so there =
is
no reason to ship a shape we already know is wrong and churn it later.

`Ctx` and `KeyContext` carried the same five fields; `Ctx`'s sixth, `cache`=
,
turned out never to be read at all, which made them identical in practice. =
One
type now serves both, with `manifest()` hanging off it.

That in turn removes two of `resolve_one`'s seven parameters: `kctx` is `ct=
x`,
and `graphblas_key` was being threaded in even though it is always readable
from `resolved_so_far` (the loop in `ensure` resolves GraphBLAS first, whic=
h is
what made the argument correct in the first place).

No behaviour change. Verified by diffing the key manifests before and after=
:
exactly one line moves, `recipe=3D` -- which is RECIPE_HASH, the compile-ti=
me
hash of this crate's own sources, and is *supposed* to move when the crate =
is
edited. Every other input (source, cc, cxx, target, openmp, patch, prejit) =
is
byte-identical, so nothing about what the artifacts are ABI-tied to changed=
.
The cost is that editing the crate invalidates all three cached deps; that =
is
the conservative direction, since it cannot produce a stale hit.

Co-authored-by: Copilot &lt;223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com&gt;

* fix: match linkme DATA sections by prefix, and give cargo-chef the crate

Two CI failures, both mine.

1. All five image builds died in `cargo chef cook` with
   &quot;failed to read /src/native-deps/Cargo.toml&quot;. chef stubs out b=
uild SCRIPTS,
   but cargo still has to RESOLVE every manifest, and graph/Cargo.toml now
   carries a path build-dependency on ../native-deps. Copying the lock file=
 in
   was not enough -- the crate itself has to be there. Copy it into both co=
ok
   stages.

2. lint/fuzz/coverage died in strip_linkme_sections with
   &quot;section '.text._RNv...6linkme...static_slice...' cannot be removed=
:
   (...register_server_events+0x256) has relocation against symbol ''&quot;=
.

   The section filter matched any token CONTAINING &quot;linkm&quot;. The r=
eal linkme
   slice sections are data sections whose names START with it
   (`linkm2_COMMANDS_LIST`, ...) -- both this branch and main have exactly =
11 of
   them. The broad match additionally caught
   `.text._RNv...6linkme...static_slice...`, a CODE section whose mangled R=
ust
   symbol merely embeds &quot;linkme&quot;, and which objcopy rightly refus=
es to remove
   because `.rela.text` relocates into it.

   Whether rustc emits that mangled function as its own section is a
   codegen-unit partitioning detail: main's archive has 359 CGUs and does n=
ot,
   this branch's has 370 and does. So the old filter was not correct-and-br=
oken,
   it was incorrect-and-lucky, and the restructure only changed the luck.
   Anchoring the match on the start of the name removes the same 11 section=
s as
   before and is independent of partitioning.

Verified by building build/runtime/Dockerfile against the PR toolchain imag=
e:
previously failed at the objcopy step, now reports
&quot;OK: libfalkordb.so is self-contained (60M)&quot;.

Co-authored-by: Copilot &lt;223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com&gt;

* fix: unbreak the lint and fuzz jobs

Both regressions were introduced by this branch.

lint: `lock --check` reads the gitlinks with `git ls-files`, and the job ru=
ns in
a container whose user differs from the checkout's owner, so git refused wi=
th
&quot;detected dubious ownership in repository at '/__w/FalkorDB/FalkorDB'&=
quot;. Mark
the workspace safe before the step. Nothing else in the job shells out to g=
it,
which is why this surfaced only now.

fuzz: the sanitizer cross-check added in the previous commit was too strict=
. It
treated &quot;-Zsanitizer=3Daddress with REDISEARCH_SAN unset&quot; as alwa=
ys wrong, but
that is a real configuration rather than a mistake: `cargo fuzz` instrument=
s the
Rust side for coverage while deliberately linking the clean RediSearch arch=
ive,
and did so before this branch existed. Downgrade that case to a cargo:warni=
ng
so the intent is still visible, and keep the hard failure for a genuine
DISAGREEMENT (both set, different values), which cannot be deliberate.

Co-authored-by: Copilot &lt;223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com&gt;

* fix(native-deps): never publish a local checkout under its pinned key

The cache key names the commit the lock pins, but a build read whatever was
checked out, so a submodule off its pin, or with uncommitted edits, was bui=
lt
and filed under the pinned key for every worktree to link. Such a checkout =
now
builds into a per-worktree entry outside the shared cache, reused while its
commit and diff are unchanged.

Making that check reliable exposed two older races: builds with different k=
eys
mutating one checkout concurrently (an IDE's cargo check and a terminal bui=
ld),
and a Ctrl-C'd build leaving its patches behind with a lock that blocked fo=
r 90
minutes. A per-checkout lock now serialises in-place builds, an on-disk jou=
rnal
undoes interrupted ones, and a lock whose holder is gone is taken over at o=
nce
(via /proc on Linux, where the slim build images have no kill binary). The =
lock
and journal live in deps/.native-deps/, so a Docker dep stage never ships t=
hem.

The recipe hash now covers only the code that shapes artifacts, so editing =
the
cache or CLI code no longer rebuilds every dep.

Closes #2315

* refactor(native-deps): use sha2 and thiserror; share one hashing module

The crate vendored a 299-line SHA-256 and hand-rolled its error type to sta=
y
dependency-free, which existed only so the Docker dep stages could run
`cargo run --offline`. Those stages now run `--locked`, which is the guaran=
tee
actually wanted, and the crate depends on sha2 and thiserror.

Hashing lives in src/hash.rs, which build.rs `include!`s for the recipe has=
h
instead of carrying its own copy of the path-plus-contents hash and the
recursive directory walk.

* refactor(native-deps): remove dead code and duplicates

copy_dir had no caller but itself. The offline mode (field, flag, env var) =
was
set by nothing, and Cache::available and indent existed only for its error.
graph/build.rs carried its own copy of util::find_archives. Also moves the
libomp search-path doc comment back onto the function it describes.

* refactor(native-deps): reduce the CLI to the two things it is for

`cargo build` needs no CLI: graph/build.rs calls native_deps::ensure. The
binary exists for the Docker dep stages, which build the deps in a layer wi=
th
no FalkorDB workspace, and for regenerating the lock file. So it now takes
dep names (default all) and reads the flavour from the same environment car=
go
build does, plus `lock [--check]` -- 58 lines, down from 285.

Gone: `key` (and the library's keys()), --dep/--san/--force/--json/--root a=
nd
the JSON output. The lock regenerate/check logic moves into lock.rs.

The macOS artifact job keyed its cache on `key --all --combined`; it now ha=
shes
the same inputs directly and falls back to an older cache via restore-keys,
which is safe because entries inside are content-addressed.

* feat(native-deps): add `native-deps prune`

The cache only ever grew: every pin bump, recipe edit or compiler change ad=
ds
a full set of entries. prune keeps the keys this checkout resolves to (and =
its
own local-build slots) plus anything resolved in the last --days (default 1=
4),
and always clears incomplete entries, stale locks and leftover .build trees=
.
--worktree also removes deps/RediSearch/bin, the in-tree RediSearch output;
--dry-run lists without removing. An entry whose lock is live is never
touched, since a build is writing it -- a dry run against a real cache caug=
ht
the first version offering to delete a running build's scratch tree.

Use is recorded by touching a .used file on resolve rather than the .stamp,
which graph/build.rs watches; touching that would rerun the build script on
every cargo build.

The macOS artifact job prunes to its current keys before saving, so a cache
restored through restore-keys does not carry superseded entries forward.

* feat(native-deps): replace gen_prejit.sh with `native-deps prejit`

The GraphBLAS knowledge moves into recipes/graphblas.rs, next to the
vendor_prejit it mirrors: the submodule's version, the JIT cache path, and
clearing and harvesting kernels with the one is_prejit_kernel filter that
vendoring already used. The flow -- clear, harvest build, workload, harvest=
,
normal build -- becomes src/prejit.rs, so the whole thing has one entry poi=
nt
and no shell script.

Two behaviour changes from the script: no interactive retry prompt (a faili=
ng
workload step is reported at the end and the run continues, as the script's
non-interactive mode did), and no second run of every suite to &quot;verify=
&quot; --
the tests pass with or without the kernels, so that run could not detect a
missing one; CI covers correctness.

* ci(codecov): ignore native-deps

It is build tooling rather than the module: it runs inside cargo build and =
the
Docker dep stages, and its unit tests live in its own workspace, which the
coverage jobs do not run -- so codecov only ever saw the lines a build scri=
pt
happened to execute, and patch coverage read 46% for code that has 26 tests=
.

* ci: drop the macOS artifact build

It ran only on manual dispatch or a verify-artifacts label, never as part o=
f a
release (release-artifacts.yml builds the Linux cells only), and in the las=
t 200
runs of build-artifacts.yml it never executed. Removing it also removes the
macOS cache-key and prune plumbing that existed only for it.

* refactor(native-deps): simplify key.rs

One manifest() instead of common() plus manifest(), a plain set() instead o=
f a
chaining one, and the key length inlined. Harvest mode now hashes the PreJI=
T
kernels like the normal flavour instead of recording 'skipped': prejit clea=
rs
them before its harvest build anyway, and prejit_harvest already separates =
the
two flavours. The manifest text is unchanged, so existing cache entries sti=
ll
hit (checked: all three deps resolved as cache hits after the change).

* fix(native-deps): review findings on keys, fingerprints and build env

- Recipes run without the variables Cargo sets for a build script or reads =
to
  steer a build (CARGO_*, RUSTFLAGS, TARGET, OUT_DIR, ...). Inherited, they
  reached RediSearch's own Cargo build and changed its output -- an
  instrumented outer build's CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS, say -- without changi=
ng
  the key. prejit's children are top-level builds and keep the environment.
- RediSearch's key includes `rustc -V`: its Rust archive bundles a std we l=
ink
  over ours with --allow-multiple-definition, which is only sound when both
  came from one compiler.
- Untracked files count as local changes: GraphBLAS's cmake globs PreJIT/*.=
c,
  tracked or not. (Binary edits already changed the fingerprint through the
  diff's index line; a test now pins that.)
- graph/build.rs: drop the duplicate llvm-23 search path and tool candidate
  left by the LLVM 23 merge, and name a command that exists in the missing-
  archive message.

* fix(native-deps): kernel advisory locks; prune and resolve review finding=
s

Build locks were O_EXCL files with a pid inside, and staleness was a guess =
--
/proc or `kill -0`, then a 90-minute age. Two waiters could both judge one =
lock
stale and both take it over, and `kill -0` reads EPERM as &quot;dead&quot; =
on macOS. They
are now flock()s (std::fs::File::lock), which the kernel releases when the
holder exits however it exits: no pid, no timeout, no takeover race. Lock f=
iles
are never deleted, since unlinking a path another process may be about to l=
ock
would let two hold it.

prune:
- skips an entry while its lock is held, and deps/RediSearch/bin while a
  RediSearch build holds its source lock (--worktree);
- reads a future timestamp as &quot;just used&quot; rather than as old;
- reports I/O errors listing the cache instead of treating it as empty, and
  reports removals that failed instead of counting them as removed.

resolve:
- .used is written only into our own cache entries, never into an override
  prefix;
- current_entries derives LAGraph's key from GraphBLAS as `ensure` resolves
  it, so a local GraphBLAS build no longer leaves prune free to delete the
  LAGraph built against it.

* fix(native-deps): journal recovery review findings

- A journal records the commit it was written against, and is discarded
  unreplayed on a checkout that has since moved: its snapshots are another
  commit's files, and restoring them would corrupt the checkout in place.
- Recovery keeps the journal when removing a file or directory the build
  added fails (anything but NotFound), so the next run retries instead of
  leaving the debris for good.
- A git failure in the local-change check -- an empty marker in a Docker
  context, with no superproject to walk up to -- reads as &quot;cannot tell=
&quot;
  rather than an error, so ensure reaches the recovery that removes it.

* fix(native-deps): recipe review findings

- RediSearch: the Rust archive is chosen by its profile dir as well as its
  name. Both flavors' outputs coexist under bin/redisearch_rs, and sorted,
  `debug-asan` comes first -- a clean build would have published the ASAN
  archive. The variant check now reads the variant dir's own name rather th=
an
  the whole path, so a checkout under a path containing &quot;asan&quot; no=
 longer
  flips the flavor. A test builds both flavors' layout under such a path.
- RediSearch removes its (empty) scratch dir like the other recipes.
- GraphBLAS checks for its archive before removing the build tree, whose cm=
ake
  logs are what explain it being missing.
- find_archives fails on an unreadable directory instead of returning nothi=
ng,
  and clear_prejit fails when it cannot remove the JIT cache, instead of
  harvesting a previous run's kernels.
- The OpenMP key includes a static libomp's prefix: the build compiles agai=
nst
  that prefix's omp.h and links its libomp.a.

* fix(native-deps): prejit review findings

- A failed run no longer leaves build/graphblas/PreJIT empty: the vendored
  kernels are backed up before they are cleared and restored if anything up=
 to
  the harvest fails.
- A missing benchmark runner (bench/pyproject.toml + uv) is an error before
  anything is cleared, not a line in the end-of-run summary: without it the
  benchmark-only kernels quietly go missing. The toolchain image now instal=
ls
  uv into its venv, so prejit runs there as is.
- FALKORDB_PREJIT_HARVEST=3D0 (or false/no/off) counts as unset, as everywh=
ere
  else in the crate.

* fix(native-deps): lock file, CLI and test-helper review findings

- `lock` honours FALKORDB_REPO_ROOT even before that checkout has a lock fi=
le,
  which is exactly when it is first run.
- from_git accepts only a gitlink (mode 160000) at stage 0, so a plain file=
 or
  a conflicted index cannot be written into the lock as a pin.
- The stale-lock message reads `rev=3D...` however it is spaced.
- The CLI rejects trailing arguments to `lock` and `prejit` -- the latter
  clears the vendored kernels and runs for a long time, so never on a typo =
--
  and a `--days` that overflows.
- graph/build.rs rejects REDISEARCH_SAN without a matching -Zsanitizer: an
  instrumented archive cannot load in an uninstrumented module.
- Test temp dirs are unique per call, not per second; dep.rs gets a test.

* docs, ci: review findings on docs, devcontainer and Docker context

- README, CLAUDE.md and the build skill set the macOS Homebrew compiler
  before the first `cargo build`, not after it: the system clang has no
  OpenMP, and GraphBLAS would silently build single-threaded.
- The cache path is documented as ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/..., whic=
h
  is what native-deps actually uses.
- CLAUDE.md: the gitlink is the authoritative pin and the lock file mirrors=
 it
  for contexts without .git; bumping a pin means staging the gitlink first,
  since `lock` reads it from the index.
- The devcontainer bakes its deps into /opt/falkordb-deps and exposes them =
as
  a read-only root. Pointing PREBUILT at the default writable cache made it=
 a
  no-op, and let a workspace build modify what the image shipped.
- .dockerignore again excludes the lagraph_lib/ and redisearch/ trees the o=
ld
  scripts left in older checkouts.
- rust-push.yml rebuilds the toolchain image on a .dockerignore change, as
  rust-pr.yml already does: it changes the build context.
- Stale names: GRAPHBLAS_LIB_DIR in a bench note, and `off` missing from
  env_flag's documented false values.

* fix(native-deps): three late review findings

- key.rs's test compared against an empty manifest, which differs whatever
  the newly added input does; it now compares the key before and after.
- The devcontainer README said LLVM 21; the image installs LLVM 23.
- `help` is no longer accepted as an undocumented alias of -h/--help.

* fix(native-deps): sanitizer Rust archive selection; drop rustc from the k=
ey

Two regressions from the review fixes, both caught by CI.

- 415e37cc9 picked RediSearch's Rust archive by looking for &quot;asan&quot=
; in its
  profile dir, from a layout I had assumed rather than observed. The real o=
ne
  (CI log, and a local Dockerfile.asan build): both flavors use the `releas=
e`
  profile, and a sanitizer build -- for which build.sh sets CARGO_BUILD_TAR=
GET
  to the host triple -- nests one level deeper, at
  redisearch_rs/&lt;triple&gt;/release/. The asan image therefore found no =
archive.
  Selection now keys on that triple level, and the test fixture uses the
  layout CI actually produced.
- 93a2c59ec put `rustc -V` in RediSearch's key. The fuzz job builds with
  nightly against the image's stable-built archive -- a pairing that ran
  green before -- and the nightly key missed the prebuilt entry and tried t=
o
  build RediSearch with no submodules checked out. rustc is out of the key.

